    "On March 23, 2023, Toshiba's board accepted a takeover proposal from a consortium led by Japan Industrial Partners (JIP), an offer of ¥4,620 per share, totaling roughly ¥2 trillion (~USD 14B) in equity value. The tender offer opened on August 8, cleared the two-thirds threshold with ~78% acceptance on September 21, and Toshiba was delisted from the Tokyo Stock Exchange's Prime Market on December 20, ending 74 years of listing history that began in 1949. The deeper story is that a six-year saga, the 2015 accounting fraud, the 2017 Westinghouse bankruptcy, the ¥600B activist-funded private placement, and four years of boardroom warfare, was resolved by a consortium containing [no foreign private equity firm]. Bain Capital had been a Round-Two finalist but withdrew amid financing and antitrust concerns. JIP + Orix + ~20 Japanese strategic LPs (Chubu Electric, Rohm Semiconductor, Suzuki, Hino Motors, NSK, Iwasaki Electric, Suzuken, among others) plus a ~¥1.2T senior loan syndicate from SMBC, Mizuho, MUFG, and Sumitomo Mitsui Trust covered the entire ¥2 trillion cleanly inside Japan.",

